        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "17a722b1-f39b-5e29-8030-c37d06615401",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: tricolour flags, the national flag of Ireland, the Irish tricolour\n- Places: France, Italy, Romania, Mexico, Ireland, the Republic of Ireland\n- Events: the French Revolution, the Revolutions of 1848, the Easter Rising\n\nFacts:\n- The flags of France, Italy, Romania, Mexico, and Ireland were first adopted with the formation of independent republics during the period from the French Revolution to the Revolutions of 1848.\n- The Irish tricolour dates from 1848, but it was not popularised until the Easter Rising in 1916 and was adopted in 1919.\n- The national flag of Ireland, called bratach na hÉireann in Irish and frequently referred to as the Irish tricolour, is the national flag and ensign of the Republic of Ireland.\n- The flag of Ireland is a vertical tricolour with green at the hoist, then white, then orange.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "efa067d1-742b-52eb-ac25-a8a41e4d6adb",
        "text": "Page: Tricolour (flag). Sentence: The flags of France, Italy, Romania, Mexico, and Ireland were all first adopted with the formation of an independent republic in the period of the French Revolution to the Revolutions of 1848, with the exception of the Irish tricolour, which dates from 1848 but was not popularised until the Easter Rising in 1916 and adopted in 1919.\nPage: Flag of Ireland. Sentence: The national flag of Ireland (Irish: \"bratach na hÉireann\" ) – frequently referred to as the Irish tricolour (\"trídhathach na hÉireann\") – is the national flag and ensign of the Republic of Ireland.\nPage: Flag of Ireland. Sentence: The flag itself is a vertical tricolour of green (at the hoist), white and orange.",
